WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.330
Welcome to the Singularity Daily, Saturday edition.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.930
Here are the last 24 hours in superintelligence, in 3 minutes.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.810
Today's about lost and found. Einstein's genius friend,

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:22.480
Kurt Gödel, left notebooks almost nobody could read.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.950
Claude, an AI, went through them in days and found 663 theorems.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.560
Meanwhile, ChatGPT got a wallet. OpenAI's agents,

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.800
called dots, can now shop and pay for you.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.600
The agent never sees your card number,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.230
and you still approve every single purchase.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.930
And Meta's agent, Muse, can now make the phone calls you don't want to make.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.910
Amazon's answer? Reportedly,

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.780
a plan to gather data on nearly every product on Earth.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.570
Over on Wall Street, Nvidia has shed about $1.6 trillion in six weeks.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.710
Its board, hunting for a new boss,

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:13.670
reportedly approached Rene Haas, who runs chip designer Arm.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.300
Rival chipmaker Cerebras jumped about 17%,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.680
on a surprise profit and a Microsoft deal.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.020
And Anthropic's giant stock debut is due next week.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.750
Its bankers reportedly turned away orders from China.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.250
Now, a long view.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:36.490
Researchers say humanoid robots could outnumber people by around 2083.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.280
That's if robots help build robots. Today, there are only about 10,000 of them.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.390
The catch? AI-powered cyberattacks.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.550
Trump's task force wants critical industries reporting them.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.950
And senators want Jensen Huang and Howard Lutnick to testify.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.370
So... is it so over?

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.450
We're so back!

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.180
Out on the road, Zoox's robotaxi,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.750
with no steering wheel, is now driving in Atlanta.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.080
Taiwan's ASUS is teaching its first humanoid

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.970
robot through cameras worn by skilled workers.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.700
And OpenAI reportedly broke up its robotics team,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.730
betting its general AI models can run robots.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.340
That's today's lost and found.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.700
Good ideas can wait eighty years for the right reader.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.670
Every day, the curve gets a little steeper.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.780
Somehow, the future keeps landing early.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.370
That's one day of news. Imagine what a year looks like.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.230
That's the Singularity Daily for today.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.120
Same time tomorrow?

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.010
Want the whole month? Hear more on November 27.
